  "query_preview": "/Through Thursday/...\nIssued at 216 PM MST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nThere is a ridge over Colorado with very light flow within it. The\nsubsidence has created a pleasant day with temperatures in the\nupper 50s to low 60s across the plains with plenty of sunshine. \n\nThe current water vapor imagery shows an elongated trough\nextending from northwest Montana to California. Models had \nrecently been showing this trough producing enough lift to \ngenerate snow showers over the mountains tomorrow afternoon and \nevening. However, the latest model runs split this trough with a \npiece staying over North Dakota and another piece moving to \nArizona tomorrow. In the middle, over Colorado, there will be \nminimal lift and limited moisture. The question is now, will there\nbe any snow showers at all? Compared to the question of, how much\nsnow will fall? The forecast was edited to show a 20-30% chance \nof snow shows in the mountains tomorrow with other locations \nremaining dry. Snow amounts will be less than 1\" and most \nlocations won't see any accumulation at all. \n\nThe Intermountain West will see a ridge develop over the region on\nSaturday that will stick around in some form through next work\nweek. The ensemble data shows almost no chance for precipitation\nin our CWA until the weekend of November 14-15. There will be very\npleasant temperatures with highs slowly warming from the low 60s\nto close to 70 later in the week across the plains. With light \nwinds, no critical fire weather conditions are expected so there \nwill be no impactful weather.",
